KS test

Based on existing code from Georg Maierhofer.

The idea was to treat the system as a Markov process, where the change
in state from time n to time n+1 is dependent entirely on the state at
time n.  Train a network to predict these differences, and apply this
as a timestepping operator in order to calculate the prediction
forward from the training domain.

What went wrong?  It took a long while to get to a "walking skeleton"
which we could then improve.  This meant that there wasn't sufficient
time to address issues such as noticing that the training data appears
to be sampled far more coarsely in time from the original CTF data.
This meant that a wider window is needed to capture the structure
movement in the training data.  When this is fixed, the prediction
also isn't good, tending to saturate.  My interpretation of this is
that it may be due to parasitic modes in the system, which there is
nothing in the training to suppress.